= Mathematics
{wiki}

= Algebra
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Number theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Primitive root
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki=Primitive_root_modulo_n}

A unit $g$ modulo $N$ is a primitive root when its multiplicative order is $\varphi(N)$.

= Quadratic residue
{parent=Number theory}
{wiki}

For an odd prime $p$, a nonzero class $a$ is a quadratic residue when $a\equiv x^2\pmod p$. These residues form an index-two subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$.

= Primitive-root lifting criterion
{parent=Number theory}

If $g$ is primitive modulo an odd prime $p$ and $g^{p-1}\not\equiv1\pmod{p^2}$, then $g$ is primitive modulo every $p^k$.

= Algebraic number theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Ring of integers of a quadratic field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki=Ring_of_integers}

For square-free $d\ne0,1$, an integral basis is $\{1,(1+\sqrt d)/2\}$ when $d\equiv1\pmod4$, and $\{1,\sqrt d\}$ otherwise. The discriminant is respectively $d$ and $4d$.

= Discriminant of a biquadratic field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

The discriminant of a biquadratic field is the product of the fundamental discriminants of its three quadratic subfields.

= Galois theory
{c}
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Galois group of a polynomial
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}
{wiki=Galois_group}

The Galois group of a separable polynomial acts faithfully on its roots; this action is transitive exactly when the polynomial is irreducible.

= Frobenius cycle type
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}

Over $\mathbb F_q$, Frobenius acts on roots of an irreducible degree-$n$ polynomial as an $n$-cycle. Factor degrees therefore give its cycle type.

= Dedekind factorization cycle test
{c}
{parent=Galois theory}

At an unramified prime, the degrees of the irreducible factors of a polynomial modulo that prime give the cycle type of an element of the Galois group.

= Representation theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Character orthogonality
{parent=Representation theory}

Irreducible characters are orthonormal for $\langle\chi,\psi\rangle=|G|^{-1}\sum_g\chi(g)\overline{\psi(g)}$, and $\sum_\chi\chi(1)^2=|G|$.

= Permutation representation
{parent=Representation theory}
{wiki}

For a finite $G$-set $X$, the character of $\mathbb C[X]$ at $g$ is $|X^g|$. Its invariant dimension is the number of $G$-orbits.

= Coding theory
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Linear code
{parent=Coding theory}
{wiki}

A binary linear $[n,k,d]$ code is a $k$-dimensional subspace of $\mathbb F_2^n$ with least nonzero weight $d$. A parity-check matrix presents it as a kernel.

= Parity extension
{parent=Coding theory}

Appending a parity bit makes every word even-weight, so an odd minimum distance $d$ becomes $d+1$ without changing code size.

= Hamming bound
{c}
{parent=Coding theory}
{wiki}

A binary length-$n$ code of size $M$ and distance at least $2t+1$ satisfies
$$
M\sum_{j=0}^t\binom nj\le2^n.
$$
Equality means the code is perfect.

= Linear algebra
{parent=Algebra}
{wiki}

= Real spectral theorem
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki=Spectral_theorem}

Every real symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. Distinct eigenspaces are orthogonal because symmetry gives $\lambda\langle u,v\rangle=\mu\langle u,v\rangle$.

= Householder reflection
{c}
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki=Householder_transformation}

For unit $v$, $I-2vv^T$ is an orthogonal reflection. Successive Householder similarities reduce a symmetric matrix while preserving symmetry.

= Power method
{parent=Linear algebra}
{wiki=Power_iteration}

Iterate $q^{(k+1)}=Aq^{(k)}/\|Aq^{(k)}\|$. With a unique dominant eigenvalue and a nonzero initial dominant component, directions converge at the spectral-ratio rate.

= Foundations of mathematics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Set theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Axiom of choice
{parent=Set theory}
{wiki}

Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function. In ZF this is equivalent to the well-ordering theorem.

= Hartogs theorem
{c}
{parent=Set theory}
{wiki=Hartogs_number}

For every set $X$ there is a least ordinal $h(X)$ that does not inject into $X$.

= Tarski cardinal-square theorem
{c}
{parent=Set theory}

In ZF, if $X\times X\cong X$ for every infinite $X$, apply this to $X\sqcup h(X)$. Tarski's comparison argument forces $X$ to inject into the ordinal $h(X)$, so $X$ is well-orderable. The converse is the well-orderable-cardinal identity $\kappa^2=\kappa$.

= Computability theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Primitive recursive function
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki}

Primitive recursive functions are generated by zero, successor, and projections under composition and primitive recursion.

= Diagonal argument
{parent=Computability theory}
{wiki}

A list $f(n,k)$ of all functions in a class closed under diagonal substitution and successor misses $d(k)=f(k,k)+1$.

= Formal language theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki=Formal_language}

= Formal grammar
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A grammar $(N,\Sigma,P,S)$ generates the terminal words reachable from $S$ by finitely many productions.

= Context-free language
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

A grammar is context-free when each production has one nonterminal on its left.

= Pumping lemma for context-free languages
{parent=Formal language theory}
{wiki}

For some $p$, every sufficiently long $w$ in a context-free language has $w=uvxyz$ with $|vxy|\le p$, $|vy|>0$, and $uv^ixy^iz$ in the language for all $i\ge0$.

= Graph theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Hall marriage theorem
{c}
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki=Hall%27s_marriage_theorem}

A bipartite graph has a matching saturating $X$ exactly when $|N(S)|\ge|S|$ for every $S\subseteq X$. Sufficiency follows by induction, splitting at a proper tight set when one exists.

= Antichain
{parent=Graph theory}
{wiki}

An antichain is a family of sets no one of which contains another.

= Statistical learning theory
{parent=Foundations of mathematics}
{wiki}

= Shattering coefficient
{parent=Statistical learning theory}

$s(H,n)$ is the largest number of label vectors induced by $H$ on $n$ points. The VC dimension is the largest $n$ with $s(H,n)=2^n$.

= VC dimension of a vector space
{c}
{parent=Statistical learning theory}
{wiki=VC_dimension}

The sign class of a $d$-dimensional real vector space of functions has VC dimension at most $d$.

= Analysis
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Topological analysis
{parent=Analysis}

= Brouwer fixed-point theorem
{c}
{parent=Topological analysis}

Every continuous self-map of a closed disc has a fixed point.

= No-retraction theorem
{parent=Topological analysis}

No closed disc retracts continuously onto its boundary. This is equivalent to <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> by the standard ray construction and the antipodal map.

= Functional analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Open mapping theorem
{parent=Functional analysis}
{wiki}

A bounded surjective linear map between Banach spaces is open. Baire category first puts a ball in the closure of an image; iterative correction removes the closure.

= Closed-range criterion for an injection
{parent=Functional analysis}

A bounded injective map between Banach spaces is bounded below exactly when its image is closed, by the bounded inverse theorem applied to its image.

= Neumann-series perturbation
{c}
{parent=Functional analysis}

If $A$ is invertible and $\|A^{-1}E\|<1$, then $A+E$ is invertible by the Neumann series. Similar iterative corrections prove stability of surjectivity.

= Density by annihilators
{parent=Functional analysis}

A linear subspace is dense exactly when the only continuous functional vanishing on it is zero, by Hahn-Banach separation.

= Fourier analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Convolution theorem
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{wiki}

For $\widehat f(\xi)=\int f(x)e^{-2\pi ix\cdot\xi}\,dx$, Fubini gives $\widehat{f*g}=\widehat f\,\widehat g$.

= Plancherel theorem
{c}
{parent=Fourier analysis}
{wiki}

The Fourier transform extends unitarily to $L^2$. For Schwartz functions, inversion and Fubini give preservation of inner products.

= Complex analysis
{parent=Analysis}
{wiki}

= Cauchy principal value
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

The principal value uses symmetric truncation at infinity and symmetric deletion around real singularities.

= Principal-value residue rule
{parent=Complex analysis}

For real $a$ and nonzero real $\omega$,
$$
\operatorname{PV}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{e^{i\omega x}}{x-a}\,dx
=i\pi\operatorname{sgn}(\omega)e^{i\omega a}.
$$

= Gamma function
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki}

$\Gamma(z)=\int_0^\infty t^{z-1}e^{-t}\,dt$ for $\Re z>0$, and $\Gamma(z+1)=z\Gamma(z)$ gives meromorphic continuation.

= Gamma reflection formula
{c}
{parent=Gamma function}

$$
\Gamma(z)\Gamma(1-z)=\frac{\pi}{\sin\pi z}.
$$

= Gamma duplication formula
{c}
{parent=Gamma function}

$$
\Gamma(z)\Gamma(z+\tfrac12)=2^{1-2z}\sqrt\pi\,\Gamma(2z).
$$
It follows by making the logarithm of the quotient entire and periodic; growth forces its periodic remainder to be constant.

= Riemann surfaces
{c}
{parent=Complex analysis}
{wiki=Riemann_surface}

= Regular covering map
{parent=Riemann surfaces}
{wiki=Covering_space}

A covering is regular when its deck group acts transitively on each fibre, equivalently when its fundamental-group subgroup is normal.

= Complete analytic function
{parent=Riemann surfaces}

A complete analytic function is the collection of all continuations of a germ. Its Riemann surface consists of these germs, with projection to their base points.

= Probability and statistics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Probability theory
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki}

= Borel-Cantelli lemmas
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Borel–Cantelli_lemma}

If $\sum\mathbb P(A_n)<\infty$, only finitely many $A_n$ occur almost surely. For independent events, divergence implies infinitely many occur. Use a union bound for the first and $\prod(1-p_n)\le e^{-\sum p_n}$ for the second.

= Characteristic-function convergence theorem
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki=Lévy%27s_continuity_theorem}

Pointwise convergence of characteristic functions to a function continuous at zero implies convergence in distribution.

= Poisson process
{c}
{parent=Probability theory}
{wiki}

A rate-$\lambda$ Poisson process has independent stationary increments. Conditional on $N_t=n$, arrival times are the order statistics of $n$ independent uniform points on $[0,t]$.

= Poisson thinning
{c}
{parent=Poisson process}
{wiki=Thinning_(probability)}

Independently retaining a point at time $s$ with probability $p(s)$ gives a Poisson count of mean $\lambda\int p(s)\,ds$.

= Information theory
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki}

= Huffman coding
{c}
{parent=Information theory}
{wiki}

Huffman's algorithm repeatedly combines the $D$ least weights for a $D$-ary prefix code and yields an optimal expected length.

= Statistical modelling
{parent=Probability and statistics}
{wiki=Statistical_model}

= Exponential family
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki}

A density $h(y)\exp\{\theta T(y)-A(\theta)\}$ has sufficient statistic $T$ and cumulant function $A$.

= Independent Poisson conditioning
{parent=Statistical modelling}

Independent Poisson variables conditioned on their sum are multinomial, with cell probabilities proportional to their rates.

= Normal linear model
{parent=Statistical modelling}

$Y=X\beta+\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, and full-rank $X$ gives $\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY$.

= Regression leverage
{parent=Statistical modelling}

For $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$, leverage is $h_{ii}$ and residual variance is $\sigma^2(1-h_{ii})$.

= Log-linear model
{parent=Statistical modelling}

Additive main effects encode mutual independence in contingency-table Poisson models. An interaction permits association of its factors.

= Maximum-likelihood estimator
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Maximum_likelihood_estimation}

An MLE maximizes sample likelihood. Regularly, $\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)\Rightarrow N(0,I(\theta)^{-1})$; parameter-dependent support can change both rate and limit.

= Gaussian conditional expectation
{c}
{parent=Statistical modelling}
{wiki=Conditional_expectation}

For jointly Gaussian $(S,Y)$ with $\operatorname{cov}(S)=V$,
$$
\mathbb E[Y\mid S]=\mathbb EY+\operatorname{cov}(Y,S)V^{-1}(S-\mathbb ES).
$$

= Mathematical physics
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Classical mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Legendre transform in mechanics
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Legendre_transformation}

For a regular Lagrangian, $p=\partial L/\partial\dot q$ and $H=p\dot q-L$. Regularity means the velocity Hessian is invertible.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For $L=m\dot r^2/2-e\phi+e\dot r\cdot A$, $p=m\dot r+eA$ and $H=|p-eA|^2/(2m)+e\phi$.

= Virial theorem
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

For a confined Newtonian gravitational system, $\dot G=2T+V$ for $G=\sum p_i\cdot r_i$, hence $2\langle T\rangle=-\langle V\rangle$.

= Integrable Hamiltonian system
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Integrable_system}

Liouville integrability means having $n$ almost-everywhere independent first integrals in mutual Poisson involution.

= Arnold-Liouville theorem
{c}
{parent=Integrable Hamiltonian system}
{wiki=Liouville–Arnold_theorem}

A compact connected regular common level of $n$ commuting integrals is an $n$-torus with local action-angle coordinates.

= Dynamical systems
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Dynamical_system}

= Strict Lyapunov function
{c}
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki=Lyapunov_function}

$V(x_0)=0$, $V>0$ elsewhere, and $\dot V<0$ prove asymptotic stability; suitable sublevel sets lie in the basin.

= Stable manifold
{parent=Dynamical systems}
{wiki}

At a hyperbolic fixed point, stable and unstable manifolds are tangent to the corresponding eigenspaces. Power-series coefficients follow from graph invariance.

= Mathematical biology
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Epidemic threshold
{parent=Mathematical biology}

A cross-infection model's disease-free equilibrium becomes a saddle when the product of infection gains exceeds the product of recovery rates.

= Cosmology
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Friedmann_equations}

$H=\dot a/a$, a fluid with $P=w\rho$ has $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$, and $\rho_{\rm crit}=3c^2H^2/(8\pi G)$.

= Phantom energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

A fluid with $w<-1$ grows in density during expansion and can produce a finite-time big-rip singularity.

= Quantum theory
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Quantum_mechanics}

= Quantum no-signalling
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=No-communication_theorem}

An uncommunicated local trace-preserving operation cannot change the other subsystem's reduced state.

= Helstrom-Holevo bound
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Helstrom_measurement}

Equiprobable pure states of overlap magnitude $c$ have optimal discrimination probability $\frac12(1+\sqrt{1-c^2})$.

= Robertson uncertainty principle
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki=Robertson_uncertainty_relation}

$\Delta A\,\Delta B\ge\frac12|\langle[A,B]\rangle|$ in units $\hbar=1$.

= Coherent state
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

$|\alpha\rangle=D(\alpha)|0\rangle$ is normalized, satisfies $A|\alpha\rangle=\alpha|\alpha\rangle$, and evolves to a phase times $|\alpha e^{-i\omega t}\rangle$.

= Born approximation
{c}
{parent=Quantum theory}
{wiki}

First-order Lippmann-Schwinger iteration gives
$$
f(\mathbf k',\mathbf k)=-\frac{m}{2\pi\hbar^2}\widetilde V(\mathbf k'-\mathbf k).
$$

= Statistical physics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki=Statistical_mechanics}

= Ideal gas free energy
{parent=Statistical physics}
{wiki=Ideal_gas}

Up to volume-independent terms, $F=-Nk_BT\log V$, so $-\partial F/\partial V=Nk_BT/V$.

= Electromagnetism
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Relativistic Lorentz force
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki=Lorentz_force}

$m\,du^\mu/d\tau=qF^\mu{}_\nu u^\nu$ gives $d\mathbf p/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ and $d(\gamma mc^2)/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$.

= General relativity
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Schwarzschild light deflection
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki=Gravitational_lens}

A null Schwarzschild geodesic with $b\gg r_s$ is deflected through $2r_s/b=4GM/(bc^2)$ to first order.

= Continuum mechanics
{parent=Mathematical physics}
{wiki}

= Boundary-layer scaling
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Boundary_layer}

Balancing $U^2/x$ with $\nu U/\delta^2$ gives $\delta\sim\sqrt{\nu x/U}$.

= Falkner-Skan equation
{c}
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Falkner–Skan_boundary_layer}

For $U=Ax^m$, $\psi=\sqrt{\nu xU}f(\eta)$ gives
$$
f'''+\frac{m+1}{2}ff''+m(1-f'^2)=0,
$$
with $f(0)=f'(0)=0$ and $f'(\infty)=1$.

= Acoustic energy flux
{parent=Continuum mechanics}
{wiki=Sound_intensity}

For harmonic acoustic amplitudes, $\langle\mathbf I\rangle=\frac12\operatorname{Re}(p\mathbf u^*)$.

= Geometry and topology
{parent=Mathematics}
{wiki}

= Algebraic topology
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Lefschetz number
{c}
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{wiki}

$L(f)=\sum_i(-1)^i\operatorname{tr}(f_*:H_i\to H_i)$, equivalently the alternating trace on simplicial chains.

= Lefschetz fixed-point theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic topology}
{wiki}

If $L(f)\ne0$, then $f$ has a fixed point. For a fixed-point-free map, sufficiently fine simplicial approximation has zero diagonal chain coefficients, hence zero Lefschetz number.

= Algebraic geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Birational variety
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{wiki=Birational_geometry}

Irreducible varieties are birational exactly when they have isomorphic function fields.

= Normalization of a nodal curve
{parent=Algebraic geometry}
{wiki=Normalization_(algebraic_geometry)}

Normalization separates a node's two branches; a rational nodal cubic has normalization $\mathbb P^1$ and two points above its node.

= Differential geometry
{parent=Geometry and topology}
{wiki}

= Curvature and torsion
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki=Curvature}

For unit speed, $\kappa=|T'|$ and $\tau=-B'\cdot N$. Generally $\kappa=|r'\times r''|/|r'|^3$ and $\tau=\det(r',r'',r''')/|r'\times r''|^2$.

= Geodesic curvature
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki}

For a surface curve, $T'=\kappa_n n+\kappa_g(n\times T)$. It is geodesic exactly when $\kappa_g=0$.

= Gauss-Bonnet theorem
{c}
{parent=Differential geometry}
{wiki=Gauss–Bonnet_theorem}

$$
\int_DK\,dA+\int_{\partial D}k_g\,ds+\sum\text{ exterior angles}=2\pi\chi(D).
$$
